  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nConditions are again nice and quiet as we welcome the new week.\nTemperatures early this morning are in the 40s and lower 50s \nwith some high clouds passing overhead. Some light rain showers \nand sprinkles developed over southern and central WI last \nevening associated with a low-mid level jet max downstream of a \nsurface cold front. The precip has stayed safely to our north \nthus far and that will remain true as it now starts to move over\ncentral Lake Michigan. Today will be very similar to yesterday \nwith afternoon temperatures in the lower and middle 70s and \nsunshine filtered by scattered high cloud cover.\n\nThat aforementioned cold front currently stretching from \ncentral IA, up across far SE MN and into northern WI won't make \na ton of eastward progress during the day. But it will pivot to \nbecome more zonally-oriented across central WI before dropping \nsouth across our area overnight tonight through tomorrow \nmorning. Weak forcing for ascent, both along the front and from \nneighboring synoptic features, and a lack of layer moisture will\nkeep any mentionable rain chances out of the forecast with this\nfrontal passage. It will, however, have a noticeable influence \non temperatures as the timing is diurnally favorable for a good \ngradient in afternoon temps tomorrow. High temperatures are \nforecast in the lower and middle 60s in our north where the \nfront will have passed by early morning, while lower and middle \n70s are expected near and south of the Kankakee and Illinois \nriver valleys where the front won't make it across until later \nin the morning.\n\nUpper 60s to lower 70s are expected on Tuesday with 70s \nforecast areawide on Wednesday. Another weak cold front looks to\ndrop into northern IL Thursday night and is then favored to \nstall nearby into the weekend. The latest NBM came in with \nslight chance and low-end chance PoPs in our north and northwest\nahead of the front Wednesday night into Thursday. However, \nforecast soundings suggest that may be difficult to achieve \nuntil some better layer moisture works in closer to the end of \nthe week. Chances are seemingly better toward the weekend near \nthe stalled baroclinicity. There is uncertainty in the exact \nevolution of this front/baroclinic zone which lowers confidence \nin temperature trends late in the week. Generally warmer- than-\nnormal conditions are favored to continue through the end of \nthe week, but a consistent signal among ensembles is for more \nseasonable temperatures for the early part of November.\n\nDoom",
